    "category": "Clear Cube Ice, FrozenFall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "Newair's 45 lb countertop clear ice maker is for people who want restaurant-style clear cubes for cocktails without a built-in undercounter machine. Newair says its FrozenFall process freezes water in layers to keep air bubbles and impurities out of the cubes, and lists up to 45 lb a day, adjustable cube thickness and ice speed, a 24-hour timer, an LCD display, a self-cleaning function, a 1-gallon water-bottle dispenser option and a two-year warranty. Newair's own pages have shown prices of $274.99, $247.49 on sale and $309.99, and one collection page listed it out of stock, so check availability. We could not find a published spec sheet with dimensions or power draw, so confirm those on the product page or manual before buying.",
    "pros": [
      "Clear, dense cubes via layered freezing, per Newair",
      "Rated up to 45 lb per day",
      "Adjustable cube thickness and 24-hour timer",
      "Two-year warranty, per Newair"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No published dimensions or power figures found",
      "Stock and pricing vary",
      "Clear cubes take longer per batch than bullet ice"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If cocktails are the point, Newair's clear ice maker is the countertop option worth buying."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Countertop Ice Makers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-countertop-ice-maker/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best countertop nugget ice maker?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people the GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra is the best nugget ice maker, with a listed 38 lb per day at 70°F, a 3 lb bin, Wi-Fi scheduling and a scale-inhibiting filter. The standard Opal 2.0 is a cheaper option rated at 24 lb per day."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do countertop ice makers keep ice frozen?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The bin is insulated but not refrigerated, so ice gradually melts back into the reservoir and is refrozen. Move ice to a freezer if you want to store it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much ice does a countertop ice maker make?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Rated output ranges from about 26 lb per day for budget bullet machines to around 44 lb per day for high-output nugget models, measured under favourable conditions. Bins usually hold about 2 to 3 lb at a time."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use tap water in an ice maker?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many manuals recommend filtered or bottled water to reduce scale and improve taste. Some warn against distilled water because the machine uses minerals to sense water level. Check your model's manual."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should I clean a countertop ice maker?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Follow the manual's schedule, typically running the cleaning cycle regularly and wiping the bin and reservoir often. Empty and dry the machine if it will sit unused for more than a few days, and descale more often in hard-water areas."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is nugget ice worth the extra cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you love soft, chewable ice and drink a lot of iced beverages, nugget machines are worth it. If you just need ice for coolers or quick drinks, a bullet ice maker at a fraction of the price is more sensible."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How loud are countertop ice makers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They hum while running and make noise as ice drops or forms. Some manufacturers list figures such as 47 dB, but real-world noise varies with placement. Scheduling features help if the machine is near living spaces."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Appliances",
    tags: [
      "best countertop ice maker",
      "nugget ice maker",
      "portable ice maker",
      "countertop nugget ice maker",
      "clear ice maker"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Countertop <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Ice Makers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Six countertop ice makers across nugget, bullet and clear ice, picked on the numbers that matter: real daily output, bin size and how easy they are to keep clean.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ge-profile-opal-2-ultra"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#ice-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Nugget, Bullet or Clear</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#output" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Daily Output and Bin Size</a>
          <a href="#placement" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Placement, Noise and Water</a>
          <a href="#cleaning" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Cleaning and Scale</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Running Cost</a>
          <a href="#vs-fridge" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Versus a Fridge or Freezer Ice Maker</a>
          <a href="#who-skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Who Should Skip One</a>
          <a href="#which-one" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Which One for Your Household</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best countertop ice maker depends first on the kind of ice you want. Nugget ice, the soft chewable kind, needs a pricier machine; bullet ice is cheap and fast but melts quickly; clear ice looks great in cocktails. Decide that first, then compare daily output and bin size, and remember that none of these machines keeps ice frozen once it is made.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Countertop ice makers all promise lots of ice, fast. The marketing numbers are real but they are measured under favourable conditions, usually a cool room and cool water, and they say nothing about bin size, how often you refill the tank, or how quickly scale builds up. We compared machines using manufacturer and retailer specifications and judged them on ice type, rated daily output, storage, refills and cleaning.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these machines. Every capacity and output figure is attributed to a manufacturer or retailer listing. Where those listings disagree, as they often do on tank size and power draw for this category, we report both rather than pick one.
          </p>

          <p>
            We covered the three main ice types because they suit different people. Nugget ice is soft and chewable and costs the most to make. Bullet ice is cheap and fast. Clear ice is about appearance and slow melting in drinks. A single machine rarely does more than one well.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also gave weight to price stability. Several of these machines, especially GE's Opal line, swing by a hundred dollars or more with rebates. Where a retailer showed a rebate with an end date, we note it and recommend checking the current price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ice-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Nugget, Bullet or Clear Ice
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nugget ice, sometimes called pellet or chewable ice, is made by scraping thin flakes of ice from a chilled cylinder and compressing them into small nuggets. It soaks up flavour, is easy to chew, and is the reason GE's Opal machines are so popular. The mechanism is more complex, which is why nugget machines cost the most.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bullet ice is made by dipping cold metal prongs into water. It forms quickly, which is why budget machines like the Igloo ICEB26 can deliver a batch in minutes. The cubes are hollow at one end and melt faster than solid cubes, which is fine for coolers and quick drinks but less good for slow sipping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear ice is made by freezing water in layers so that air and impurities are pushed out, the approach Newair calls FrozenFall. The result is dense, transparent cubes that melt slowly and look good in a glass. Expect slower production per batch than bullet ice.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ice type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Texture</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Melt speed</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical price class</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nugget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soft, chewable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fast</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bullet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hollow, firm</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fast</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear cube</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dense, solid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Middle</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Pick the ice type first, then the machine. Most dissatisfaction with countertop ice makers comes from buying a bullet machine and expecting nugget ice, or vice versa.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We researched these six from manufacturer pages and retailer specification listings; we did not lab-test them. Every output and capacity figure is attributed to its source, and where listings disagree we say so. Prices and rebates change often, so check the current listing.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
