        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best countertop nugget ice maker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people the GE Profile Opal 2.0 Ultra is the best nugget ice maker, with a listed 38 lb per day at 70°F, a 3 lb bin, Wi-Fi scheduling and a scale-inhibiting filter. The standard Opal 2.0 is a cheaper option rated at 24 lb per day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do countertop ice makers keep ice frozen?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The bin is insulated but not refrigerated, so ice gradually melts back into the reservoir and is refrozen. Move ice to a freezer if you want to store it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much ice does a countertop ice maker make?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rated output ranges from about 26 lb per day for budget bullet machines to around 44 lb per day for high-output nugget models, measured under favourable conditions. Bins usually hold about 2 to 3 lb at a time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use tap water in an ice maker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many manuals recommend filtered or bottled water to reduce scale and improve taste. Some warn against distilled water because the machine uses minerals to sense water level. Check your model's manual.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I clean a countertop ice maker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the manual's schedule, typically running the cleaning cycle regularly and wiping the bin and reservoir often. Empty and dry the machine if it will sit unused for more than a few days, and descale more often in hard-water areas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is nugget ice worth the extra cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you love soft, chewable ice and drink a lot of iced beverages, nugget machines are worth it. If you just need ice for coolers or quick drinks, a bullet ice maker at a fraction of the price is more sensible.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud are countertop ice makers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They hum while running and make noise as ice drops or forms. Some manufacturers list figures such as 47 dB, but real-world noise varies with placement. Scheduling features help if the machine is near living spaces.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

